WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.984 --> 00:00:13.332
Hi, Little Fox readers!

2
00:00:14.232 --> 00:00:18.756
I have to go downstairs soon and help my little brother with his science project.

3
00:00:19.597 --> 00:00:24.561
But first I want to tell you about another really cool landmark: Pompeii.

4
00:00:25.601 --> 00:00:31.086
It's an ancient city on the west coast of Italy that is one of the oldest and largest

5
00:00:31.246 --> 00:00:33.408
archaeological sites in the world.

6
00:00:34.428 --> 00:00:38.971
Unfortunately it's also famous because of a terrible tragedy.

7
00:00:39.972 --> 00:00:44.835
In 79 AD it was totally wiped out by a volcano!

8
00:00:45.775 --> 00:00:52.399
Pompeii was built right at the foot of Mount Vesuvius sometime during the sixth century BC.

9
00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:54.140
I asked Mr.

10
00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:59.564
Fay why the people of Pompeii built their city so close to an active volcano.

11
00:01:00.596 --> 00:01:06.942
Mr. Fay said they had never seen Mount Vesuvius erupt so they just assumed it would be safe.

12
00:01:07.962 --> 00:01:08.843
But it wasn't.

13
00:01:10.284 --> 00:01:13.888
Mount Vesuvius had been quiet for almost a thousand years.

14
00:01:14.788 --> 00:01:20.873
But when a volcano goes without erupting for a long time like that, pressure builds up inside it.

15
00:01:21.914 --> 00:01:28.160
On the morning of August 24, this built-up pressure finally caused a massive eruption.

16
00:01:29.172 --> 00:01:36.017
Mr. Fay told us about a Roman writer named Pliny the Younger who recorded what happened that day.

17
00:01:37.038 --> 00:01:43.103
According to Pliny, a towering cloud of smoke and volcanic debris filled the sky.

18
00:01:44.124 --> 00:01:50.208
Hot rocks rained down on the city all day long as people hid inside buildings.

19
00:01:51.129 --> 00:01:54.772
The next morning there was a huge explosion.

20
00:01:55.836 --> 00:02:01.419
That's when a deadly wave of cinders and burning-hot gas swept over Pompeii.

21
00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:09.804
The whole city, including thousands of people, was buried under six meters of volcanic ash!

22
00:02:11.385 --> 00:02:16.968
I bet you're wondering the same thing I was wondering: Why is an ancient city that was completely

23
00:02:17.008 --> 00:02:20.650
destroyed by a volcano still a famous landmark?

24
00:02:21.671 --> 00:02:31.230
Well, in 1748, after Pompeii had been buried for 17 centuries, the king of Spain began organizing digs

25
00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:34.191
there, hoping to find valuable artwork.

26
00:02:35.291 --> 00:02:39.872
Soon others began digging too—and taking whatever they found.

27
00:02:41.033 --> 00:02:49.255
Finally, in 1860, an archaeologist named Giuseppe Fiorelli put a stop to all the careless digging by developing

28
00:02:49.295 --> 00:02:52.296
a new system for keeping track of what was uncovered.

29
00:02:53.308 --> 00:02:57.452
Fiorelli also made sure that the buildings were handled more carefully.

30
00:02:58.453 --> 00:03:04.498
For the first time, workers dug out the buildings by starting at the top and working their way

31
00:03:04.578 --> 00:03:09.082
down. This was a much better way of preserving what was found.

32
00:03:10.683 --> 00:03:15.147
Some workers at the site discovered open cavities in the hardened ash and mud.

33
00:03:16.047 --> 00:03:21.192
They realized that these were spaces left behind after buried bodies decayed.

34
00:03:22.184 --> 00:03:27.526
So Fiorelli came up with a clever idea—to pour plaster into these spaces.

35
00:03:28.667 --> 00:03:36.230
Once the plaster dried, workers could chip away the hardened ash and mud, revealing casts of the buried

36
00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:43.132
people. If you ask me, these casts are amazing, but pretty creepy too.

37
00:03:44.113 --> 00:03:49.815
You can tell from looking at the casts that the people were crouching down or clinging to family

38
00:03:49.875 --> 00:03:51.616
members when they were buried alive.

39
00:03:52.768 --> 00:03:56.731
And you can see in the people's faces just how scared they were.

40
00:03:58.192 --> 00:04:03.816
Because of Fiorelli's work, scholars have learned a lot about daily life in ancient Pompeii.

41
00:04:04.897 --> 00:04:13.924
It was once a bustling, prosperous city with temples, theaters, bathhouses, restaurants, and factories for making wool and

42
00:04:13.984 --> 00:04:22.542
tools. And get this: workers at the site also found bakeries—with bread still sitting in the ovens!

43
00:04:23.722 --> 00:04:32.187
Inscriptions on buildings, tablets, tombs, and the bases of statues gave scholars even more information about life in

44
00:04:32.247 --> 00:04:38.171
Pompeii. A banker's tablets revealed how people made business transactions.

45
00:04:39.292 --> 00:04:46.536
Writings on walls supplied details about things like politics, gladiator battles—even falling in love!

46
00:04:48.076 --> 00:04:51.539
Today Pompeii is open to the public all year long.

47
00:04:52.519 --> 00:04:58.143
Mr. Fay said that it's one of Italy's most popular tourist attractions, and that going there is like

48
00:04:58.223 --> 00:04:59.544
stepping back in time.

49
00:05:00.565 --> 00:05:04.948
You can see some of the plaster casts that were made from buried victims.

50
00:05:05.989 --> 00:05:11.793
You can also stroll along the narrow streets and alleyways, just as people did two thousand years ago,

51
00:05:12.374 --> 00:05:15.276
and look at the ancient stone walls and columns.

52
00:05:16.512 --> 00:05:22.015
I read that you can go inside many of the buildings too, and imagine what it was like

53
00:05:22.095 --> 00:05:27.839
to live in one of the houses, eat at a restaurant, or perform in a theater!

54
00:05:29.419 --> 00:05:36.063
I learned that Pompeii is so well-preserved because it was buried for such a long time under hardened

55
00:05:36.363 --> 00:05:37.044
ash and mud.

56
00:05:37.944 --> 00:05:46.093
But unfortunately, after being constantly exposed to crowds of tourists and harsh weather, Pompeii has suffered a lot

57
00:05:46.113 --> 00:05:46.733
of damage.

58
00:05:47.713 --> 00:05:53.316
An entire building, the House of the Gladiators, completely collapsed in 2010.

59
00:05:54.396 --> 00:06:01.199
Now all that's left of this ancient site, where mighty gladiators once prepared for battle, is a huge

60
00:06:01.279 --> 00:06:02.260
pile of rubble.

61
00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:04.821
I think that's really sad.

62
00:06:05.801 --> 00:06:11.784
I wanted to know if people living near Pompeii today have to worry about Mount Vesuvius erupting again.

63
00:06:12.796 --> 00:06:14.878
After all, it's still a volcano!

64
00:06:16.279 --> 00:06:23.785
Mr. Fay said that Mount Vesuvius has erupted many times since August 24, 79 AD, but never as

65
00:06:23.865 --> 00:06:25.326
violently as on that day.

66
00:06:26.287 --> 00:06:29.149
And today scientists know a lot more about volcanoes.

67
00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:38.877
Before a volcano erupts, there are usually warning signs—like a water supply drying up or an earthquake—so scientists

68
00:06:38.917 --> 00:06:41.960
can monitor volcanoes for any dangerous activity.

69
00:06:42.816 --> 00:06:45.697
Whew! I felt a lot better after Mr.

70
00:06:45.737 --> 00:06:46.858
Fay told us this.

71
00:06:47.858 --> 00:06:53.441
If Mount Vesuvius were about to erupt again, people could be warned months ahead of time.

72
00:06:54.301 --> 00:06:57.002
Well, I hope you enjoyed learning about Pompeii.

73
00:06:57.803 --> 00:06:58.663
I know I did!

74
00:06:59.543 --> 00:07:01.204
I've got to go help my brother now.

75
00:07:02.184 --> 00:07:05.406
He's making something really cool for a science project.

76
00:07:06.186 --> 00:07:07.047
Guess what it is?

77
00:07:07.927 --> 00:07:10.048
That's right; a volcano!
